WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.009
It's been said that you can't please everyone
all of the time.

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:06.150
That's especially true for movie endings.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.519
Hollywood history is full of shocking finales
that have left people confused, made moviegoers

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:13.889
mad, or sparked fierce debate between critics.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:18.320
From decades-old films to modern-day blockbusters,
here are some of the most controversial movie

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.980
endings of all-time.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.359
Major spoilers ahead, obviously.

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:24.130
Monty Python's Life of Brian

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.670
Want a surefire way to stir up controversy?

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:27.970
Make a movie about Jesus.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.550
It worked for Mel Gibson, Martin Scorsese,
and of course, Monty Python.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.010
Okay, technically Life of Brian isn't about
Jesus at all.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:40.780
It's about a guy named Brian who gets mistaken
for a messiah and ends up getting crucified.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.260
And while our reluctant hero dies a painful
death, all the guys hanging on crosses around

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.010
him launch into an upbeat musical number.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:55.370
"Always look on the bright side of life!"

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.710
Needless to say, Life of Brian made some people
pretty upset.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.500
Protesters sang hymns outside movie theaters,
and the film was banned in some countries

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.540
for decades.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.780
The Catholic Church jumped in and made up
their own rating for Life of Brian: a "C"

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:09.780
for Condemned.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.780
Harsh.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.610
But despite the notoriety — or more likely
because of it — Life of Brian has since

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.790
become a comedy classic.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.790
Titanic

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.729
Sure, Titanic is still the second highest-grossing
movie of all-time, but those billions of dollars

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:25.330
couldn't save the film from endless scrutiny.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.520
According to countless fans across the world,
the ending of Titanic is nothing short of

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.700
a disaster.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.829
After the boat goes down, Jack and Rose find
themselves stuck in the freezing cold waters

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:35.829
of the Atlantic.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.840
Then, Jack freezes to death.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.820
It's this scene that's sparked most of outrage.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.010
Wasn't there room on that piece of wood for
the both of them?

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:48.560
The debate was so intense that in 2012, Mythbusters
dedicated an entire episode to discovering

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.560
the answer.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.149
With the help of director James Cameron, they
determined that if Rose had just tied her

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:56.950
life jacket to the underside of the door,
there would've been enough buoyancy to hold

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:57.950
them both.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.870
But Cameron doesn't buy into that argument,
saying that, "The script says Jack dies, he

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:02.899
has to die.

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:03.899
Maybe we screwed up.

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:06.009
The board should have been a tiny bit smaller.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:07.170
But the dude's going down."

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.720
In other words, true love is a lie and you
should never hope for happiness.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.120
Geez, Cameron...

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.150
Blade Runner

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:18.720
By this point, there are seven different versions
of Blade Runner available to watch, and thanks

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.080
to all that tinkering, sci-fans have spent
countless hours discussing whether or not

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.580
Rick Deckard is actually a replicant.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:26.780
So what's the correct answer?

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.440
Well, if you go by the original ending, it's
a straightforward "no."

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.580
The theatrical cut ends on an upbeat note,
with Deckard and his robotic lady love escaping

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.290
into the mountains to live a long and happy
life.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.190
However, that all changed with the Director's
Cut.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.540
That's the version in which Scott added Deckard's
unicorn dream and cut the happy Hollywood

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.540
ending.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:51.050
This time around, the film ends with our protagonist
finding Gaff's origami message, implying the

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.740
cop knows what Deckard dreams about — because
Deckard's memories are implants, thus making

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:56.740
him a replicant.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.170
Ever since, people have argued about Deckard's
true nature.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.810
Even Ridley Scott and Harrison Ford don't
agree.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.520
The director firmly believes Deckard is a
machine, while the actor fought to keep his

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.440
character 100 percent human.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:14.950
"So I resisted the idea of being a replicant,
I suppose, as a replicant would."

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.860
That settles it then.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.300
Or does it…

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.030
The Mist

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.700
The ending of The Mist is one of the bleakest
finales ever put to film.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.030
Directed by Frank Darabont, The Mist focuses
on a group of New Englanders trapped in a

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.340
grocery store by a mysterious fog.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:35.030
The titular mist is full of horrifying monsters,
but things aren't so great inside the store

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.030
either.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.040
Once people start getting sacrificed, Thomas
Jane's character, David Drayton, makes a break

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.400
for freedom with a small band of survivors,
but unfortunately, there's no happy ending

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.400
here.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.200
Lost in the mist with no hope in sight, David
performs a mercy killing to spare his friends

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.000
— and his own son — from the oncoming
creatures.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.370
And then, just a few painful seconds later,
a group of army guys show up to save the day

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.070
and look for survivors.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.900
Yeah, it's bleak, grim, and completely different
from Stephen King's novella, but the author

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.520
loved Darabont's twist, saying, "Frank wrote
a new ending that I loved.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.410
It is the most shocking ending ever."

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:10.950
Watchmen

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.760
Even though Alan Moore's Watchmen is widely
considered the greatest graphic novel ever

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.760
written, it took quite awhile to get a movie
adaptation up on the silver screen.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.340
At one point, Terry Gilliam took a crack at
the material, but when that didn't pan out,

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.420
it eventually fell to Zack Snyder to get the
thing made.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:30.950
The result was a film that was pretty faithful
to the book…except for one giant, major

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:31.950
change.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.920
In Moore's original story, the villainous
Ozymandias destroys New York with a mocked

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.190
up alien squid.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:41.730
The arrival of the monster convinces the world's
superpowers that extraterrestrials are going

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:47.290
to attack, forcing the US and the USSR to
put aside their differences and band together

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.650
in order to face the otherworldly foe.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.190
However, Snyder totally dropped the whole
alien sea beast angle.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:58.370
Instead, Ozymandias blows up New York and
frames the omnipotent Dr. Manhattan, tricking

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:03.550
America and the Soviet Union into becoming
friends to defeat the naked blue superhero.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:11.500
"Millions of lives were suddenly ended, in
an act of evil perpetrated by Dr. Manhattan

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.500
himself."

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.930
Snyder has since said that the squid was cut
out so more time could be spent building the

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.190
characters of Manhattan and Rorschach.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.500
For his part, he's also acknowledged that
the altered ending was "the biggest knock

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.960
against the movie."

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.620
So there's that.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.340
The Dark Knight Rises

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:31.590
Christopher Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy was
a game-changer for superhero movies, and,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:35.920
as the final film in the series,The Dark Knight
Rises had a lot to live up to.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.840
But instead of diving headfirst into the darkness,
the third Nolan film ended on a much happier

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.780
note, with the Caped Crusader saving Gotham
from a nuclear bomb before giving up his vigilante

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.150
ways to travel the world with Selina Kyle.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:53.140
As a result, many fans wondered if this was
some sort of Inception-style dream sequence.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:57.700
After all, the scene plays out exactly like
a fantasy described by Alfred earlier in the

00:05:57.700 --> 00:05:58.220
film.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:07.460
"I had this fantasy that would look across
the tables and I'd see you there with a wife."

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.620
Christian Bale, on the other hand, insist
Batman's happy ending is completely real:

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.010
"My personal opinion is no, it was not a dream,
that that was for real."

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.650
Some people thought the movie should've gone
in a more depressing direction by killing

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:25.139
the superhero, while others were glad that
Bruce Wayne finally got a happy ending.

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:29.200
And really, we shouldn't have been surprised
by the film's upbeat finale because, as Harvey

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.990
Dent once explained it:

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.100
"The night is darkest just before the dawn."

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.370
This was the dawn audiences had been promised.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.639
The Grey

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:43.300
Set in Alaska, The Grey follows a hunter named
John Ottway, played by Liam Neeson, who's

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.150
stranded in the wilderness with a group of
oil workers.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.300
While he starts the movie suicidal, Ottway
slowly regains the will to live — which

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.240
is too bad since he winds up in the middle
of a wolf den.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:57.762
Ready to fight, Ottway tapes a knife and broken
bottles to his hand, but before the final

00:06:57.762 --> 00:07:00.340
confrontation can start, the screen cuts to
black.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:01.180
Movie over.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:01.800
"WHAT?!"

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.460
"WHAT?!"

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.180
"Whaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa…."

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.260
Audiences were sharply divided.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.830
Some people though the ending was perfect,
while others felt cheated.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:16.730
As for director Joe Carnahan, he cut the wolf-fighting
finale because the film had already reached

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:21.640
its "emotional conclusion," and he also worried
that the sight of CG canines might ruin the

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:22.640
scene.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.790
However, Carnahan admits the promotional material
"was the enemy of the film in a lot of ways.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:31.700
I guess it created a promise that people felt
the movie didn't live up to."

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:33.350
Snowpiercer

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:37.919
Directed by Bong Joon-ho, Snowpiercer is set
in the middle of a wintry post-apocalypse

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:41.560
with the last of humanity on a train that
travels around the world.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.770
Unfortunately, living conditions aren't a
picture of equality.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:49.139
Folks in the tail end are treated like prisoners,
while the people in the front live like kings.

00:07:49.139 --> 00:07:53.350
So a group of rebels, led by Curtis, played
by Chris Evans, fights their way up to the

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.510
front of the train, hoping to overthrow the
head honchos.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:08:01.260
But when Curtis makes his way into the engine,
he learns the train operates using child slave

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.260
labor.

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.820
Realizing the entire system is corrupt and
impossible to save, Curtis destroys the train,

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:10.020
possibly killing everyone on board, except
for two kids.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.020
Emerging from the rubble, a teenage girl and
a young boy find themselves Adam and Eve of

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.260
a frozen world.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:19.590
And as they step into the snow, they see a
polar bear, a sign the world is thawing out

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.100
and that maybe there's hope after all.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.770
Well, that's what Bong Joon-ho intended anyway,
saying, "It's a very hopeful ending...those

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:27.780
two kids will spread the human race."

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.650
But many people had trouble getting past certain
plot issues.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:34.990
As plenty of critics have pointed out, those
two kids have lived their entire lives on

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:38.010
the train, so they'd have no hope of surviving
the frigid wasteland.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.610
That is, if that big ol' bear didn't eat them.

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.390
He's gotta be pretty hungry.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:44.390
Passengers

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:48.640
On paper, Passengers should've been one of
the biggest blockbusters of 2016.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.510
It starred Chris Pratt and Jennifer Lawrence
— two of the biggest stars in the business

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.180
— and it was based on a screenplay that
many hailed as one of the best scripts in

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.180
Hollywood.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:02.820
But the sci-fi flick was a flop, grossing
$100 million domestically on a $110 million

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:03.820
budget.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:05.839
So what went so terribly wrong aboard Passengers?

00:09:05.839 --> 00:09:10.420
Well, moviegoers were expecting to see a story
about two beautiful people who accidentally

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:13.600
wake up from suspended animation while traveling
to a new planet.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.900
Unable to return to their pods, the two are
fated to spend their lives together on the

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:18.900
spaceship.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.900
But the trailers were more than slightly misleading.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:25.070
As it turns out, Chris Pratt's character,
Jim, wakes up after his pod malfunctions.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:29.980
Unwilling to spend his life alone, he selfishly
releases Jennifer Lawrence's character, Aurora,

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:31.110
against her will.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.010
Even the robot bartender was surprised.

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.140
"Who's the lucky lady?"

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.540
"This is Aurora."

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:37.540
"Aurora…"

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:41.380
Admittedly, the movie could've done something
interesting with this premise.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:45.880
But instead of dealing with Jim's decision
in a mature, complex way, the filmmakers have

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:49.990
Aurora fall in love with the guy and decide
to spend her life with him…even though he's

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:52.090
essentially kidnapped and murdered her.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:56.810
When the rest of the passengers wake up nearly
90 years later, they find that Jim and Aurora

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:00.190
lived a long, happy life and grew a bunch
of trees on their spaceship.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:04.830
As a result, critics hit the movie hard, and
audiences decided to stay away from theaters.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.120
Thanks for watching!

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:09.670
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:11.930
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

